# Target C-II — convex-route upgrade of the degree, matching, and train-support stack

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proved proof-only composition; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** current low-`t` full-core Type-II endpoint  
**New input:** merged convex Section-63 route theorem (`11b96ae4`)  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The merged convex-route theorem halves the angular-deletion loss in the global
straight-event count:

\[
S_\beta\ge x_B-{d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta}.
\]

At the `15`-degree window used by the degree/phase-gate and matching theorems, this
raises the straight-vertex floor from `1.1543116921...D` to

\[
\boxed{1.2389498413637606\ldots D-o(D)}.
\]

Because every later argument in the branch depends only on the straight population,
physical repetition surplus, signed injectivity, or the stopped-train singleton
budget, the improvement propagates without changing their geometry. In particular:

- at least `1.1930133014...D` straight vertices are simple-outer;
- at least `0.6194749206...D` signed penetration contacts can be chosen pairwise
  vertex-disjoint;
- at least `0.3227175723...D` signed penetrations lie on non-singleton strict-rhombus
  trains;
- at least `0.1613587861...D` of those admit a physical matching;
- after restricting to simple-outer tails, the train-supported matching still has
  size at least `0.1383905162...D`.

This note records the exact propagation so the frontier is not left at the older
pre-convex-route constants.

---

## 1. Convex-route straight-event floor at 15 degrees

Set

\[
\beta={\pi\over12},
\qquad
c_{15}:=1-\cos(\pi/12)
=1-{\sqrt6+\sqrt2\over4}.
\tag{CU.1}
\]

The merged convex-route theorem proves

\[
\boxed{
S_{15}\ge x_B-{d_{out}\over c_{15}}.
}
\tag{CU.2}
\]

On the low-`t` subsequence, with

\[
t={q-k\over D},\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D},
\]

near-disk perimeter convergence gives

\[
{x_B\over D}=\pi+\delta-t-\kappa+o(1).
\]

Hence

\[
\liminf {S_{15}\over D}
\ge
\pi-t-\kappa-
\left({1\over c_{15}}-1\right)\delta.
\tag{CU.3}
\]

The current low-`t` network squeeze is

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}\le R(t),
\qquad
R(t_{42})=0.00576795.
\tag{CU.4}
\]

Since

\[
{1\over c_{15}}-1
=28.3477402704905\ldots>4,
\]

the penalty in (CU.3) is maximized at

\[
\delta={R(t)\over2},\qquad \kappa=0.
\]

At

\[
t=t_{42}=1.8208886379794447041\ldots,
\]

we obtain

\[
\boxed{
{S_{15}\over D}
\ge1.2389498413637606406\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{CU.5}

The degree-collapse theorem is unchanged: every `15`-degree straight internal
occurrence is unloaded and has contact degree exactly three or four. Its geometric
straight-occurrence injection also remains unchanged, so (CU.5) counts distinct
physical vertices.

---

## 2. Simple-outer population

The independently merged near-simple outer-walk theorem gives

\[
{b-V_{out}\over D}
\le0.0459365399110338116\ldots+o(1).
\tag{CU.6}
\]

Every straight physical vertex with another outer-face occurrence contributes at
least one unit to `b-V_out`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
{V_{simp,15}\over D}
\ge
1.2389498413637606406
-0.0459365399110338116
-o(1)
}
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{
{V_{simp,15}\over D}
\ge1.1930133014527268290\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{CU.7}

All local simple-outer phase-gate classifications from the earlier theorem apply to
this larger population without modification.

---

## 3. All-extra signed penetration count and matching

Let `V_3,V_4` be the degree-three and degree-four straight populations. Then

\[
V_3+V_4=S_{15}.
\]

A cubic straight vertex has one extra contact and a quartic straight vertex has two.
The merged signed-normal theorem, strengthened by the branch audit, makes **all** of
these extra incidences globally edge-injective. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
E_{all,15}=V_3+2V_4=S_{15}+V_4.
}
\tag{CU.8}

Every edge has signed inward component greater than `1/sqrt(2)` relative to its
associated hull-side normal.

The endpoint-multiplicity theorem proved on this branch is independent of the old
straight-count constant: the opposite endpoint of a penetration receives at most two
such contacts, and a penetration cannot terminate at another `15`-degree straight
vertex. Thus the penetration graph is bipartite of maximum degree two. It follows that
its edge set splits into two matchings and

\[
\boxed{
{M_{15}\over D}
\ge {1\over2}{E_{all,15}\over D}
\ge0.6194749206818803203\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{CU.9}

This is a pairwise vertex-disjoint physical matching of strongly signed inward unit
contacts.

The exact degree/edge identity

\[
E_{all,15}+V_3=2S_{15}
\tag{CU.10}
\]

also yields the convenient split

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{either}&V_3/D\ge0.6194749206818803203\ldots-o(1),\\[1mm]
\text{or}&E_{all,15}/D\ge1.8584247620456409609\ldots-o(1).
\end{array}
}
\tag{CU.11}

No use is made of the numerically nearby but unrelated common-cap `t` thresholds.

---

## 4. Propagation through the singleton stopped-train budget

Use the independently audited conservative rhombic-train floor

\[
{T_R\over D}
\ge1.9050256137279146917\ldots-o(1)
\tag{CU.12}
\]

rather than the newer `0.00042` proof-only refinement. The low-`t` topology theorem
gives

\[
{T\over D}
\le2.8212578827176370437\ldots+o(1).
\tag{CU.13}
\]

With the exact split `T=T_R+S`, where `S` is the singleton stopped-train population,

\[
\boxed{
{S\over D}
\le0.9162322689897223521\ldots+o(1).
}
\tag{CU.14}

At most `S` distinct signed penetration edges can be singleton trains. Combining
(CU.5), (CU.8), and (CU.14),

\[
\boxed{
{E_{R,15}\over D}
\ge0.3227175723740382885\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{CU.15}

where every counted edge lies on a non-singleton strict-rhombus train.

Restricting first to one signed extra contact from each simple-outer straight vertex,
all selected contacts are still globally distinct. Equations (CU.7) and (CU.14) give

\[
\boxed{
{E_{R,simp,15}\over D}
\ge0.2767810324630044769\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{CU.16}

---

## 5. Train-supported physical matchings

Deleting penetration edges cannot increase either side's degree in the penetration
graph. Therefore the maximum-degree-two bipartite matching argument applies to the
train-supported subfamilies as well.

From (CU.15),

\[
\boxed{
{M_{R,15}\over D}
\ge0.1613587861870191442\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{CU.17}

From (CU.16),

\[
\boxed{
{M_{R,simp,15}\over D}
\ge0.1383905162315022384\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{CU.18}

Thus more than `0.13839D` pairwise vertex-disjoint signed penetrations simultaneously
have

1. a simple-outer degree-three/four tail;
2. inward component greater than `1/sqrt(2)`; and
3. membership in a non-singleton strict-rhombus train.

This is the cleanest current deformation-ready subfamily.

---

## 6. Terminal-versus-internal train edge split

Every edge in the simple-outer train-supported family is either a terminal edge of its
rhombic train or an internal train edge shared by two strict rhombi. Thus one of the
two classes contains at least half of (CU.16):

\[
\boxed{
{\max\{E_{term},E_{int}\}\over D}
\ge0.1383905162315022384\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{CU.19}

If the terminal branch holds, each non-singleton train has only two terminal edges, so
at least

\[
\boxed{
0.0691952581157511192\ldots D-o(D)
}
\tag{CU.20}

distinct rhombic trains are hit by these signed penetration terminals.

If the internal branch holds, at least `0.1383905162D-o(D)` signed simple-outer
penetrations lie strictly inside rhombic train tracks. This is now the stronger live
strip-occupancy branch.

---

## 7. Updated frontier

The convex-route theorem materially strengthens the branch rather than merely changing
a decimal:

```text
>= 1.2389498413 D distinct 15-degree straight unloaded degree-3/4 vertices
>= 1.1930133014 D of them simple-outer
>= 0.6194749206 D pairwise vertex-disjoint signed penetrations
>= 0.3227175723 D signed penetrations on non-singleton rhombic trains
>= 0.1613587861 D train-supported penetration matching
>= 0.1383905162 D simple-outer train-supported penetration matching
```

The remaining obstruction is still macroscopic strip occupancy. A one-rhombus-thick
convex boundary strip can carry linearly many parallel penetration edges on one train,
so an `O(1)` occupancy lemma is false. Closure must instead show that a train carrying
macroscopic matching mass either

- spends enough boundary turning / phase defect to violate the low-`t` budgets,
- creates a coherent strip to which the finite oblique-lattice or shear competitor can
  be applied, or
- requires more independent diameter bracing than the near-extremal Woodall graph can
  provide.

**C-II remains open.**

---

## 8. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_convex_upgrade_degree_matching_train.py
```

for all displayed endpoint constants.
